> > > > > Sorry, I directed you to the wrong work by Green and Schickler. I actually meant to refer to their book Partisan Hearts and Minds: https://books.google.com/books?hl=en&lr=&id=HVY18n59ThgC&oi=fnd&pg=PP9&dq=schickler+and+green+stability+partisanship&ots=cMtmaXUxmx&sig=0ydUnOXV4mfHfgEMHSSUZERdJV8#v=onepage&q=schickler%20and%20green%20stability%20partisanship&f=false <https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://books.google.com/books?hl=en&lr=&id=HVY18n59ThgC&oi=fnd&pg=PP9&dq=schickler*and*green*stability*partisanship&ots=cMtmaXUxmx&sig=0ydUnOXV4mfHfgEMHSSUZERdJV8*v=onepage&q=schickler*20and*20green*20stability*20partisanship&f=false__;KysrKyMlJSUl!!Mih3wA!XXrXPpm22V_0aDy8V3qTtHMfImn_ki7y-nOvsCtC-mKr6vyThAHDV2j3M1KHTdB9$>. They implement an autoregressor model on a continuous variable (chapter 3). But they don't use SEM at all. They employ the Anderson and Hsiao (1981) and Arellano (1989) estimators. It's a little outdated, so that's why I wanted to implement a measurement model, as used by the more recent papers I cited in the previous email.
> > > > >
> > > > > So, to summarize, the autoregressor model is really what I'm looking for. I understand that a three-wave panel may be too short for me to draw inferences from, but, in actuality, I'm analyzing a bunch of different panels, some of them with many more waves. I'm just starting from the simplest possible case. After that, I should be able to expand the code myself to panels with additional waves.
> > > > >
> > > > > So, to recap, here's the data I have: a three-wave panel in which I'm interested in a continuous variable. I want to use an autoregressor model to get at how much this variable changes over time. Does that make sense?
